I was using streams and consumer groups to consume payments “in queue,” but in addition to being too costly, I noticed the same could be achieved with KeyDB queues.  
Therefore, we will update the code to use KeyDB queues instead of streams and consumer groups.  

Our proxy app will perform an LPUSH to the payment queue, and the worker will perform an RPOP to process the payments.  

This way, the worker will process the payments in the order they were received, rather than in parallel as was done with consumer groups.  
We will also change the `amount` values from float32 to float64 due to an inconsistency that was occurring with payment amounts.  

